  "what_the_study_did": "The study performed a pancancer analysis of human and dog cancer samples to quantify transcriptomic similarities.",
  "what_it_found": "Overall, dogs are an excellent model for many human cancers, and tend to be better models of adult cancers than pediatric cancers, with some exceptions.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Dogs are particularly useful for comparative genomics studies as they share human diet and environment, and, from a genomics perspective, dogs are closely related to humans.",
      "topic": "Dog usefulness"
    },
    {
      "quote": "Dogs develop spontaneous tumors that closely resemble human tumors, have a high tumor incidence rate, and their popularity as pets ensures the wide availability of samples for scientific studies.",
      "topic": "Dog tumor resemblance"
    },
    {
      "quote": "Individual cancer studies reveal both similarities and differences between species, but the extent of the molecular similarity across tumor types remains unclear.",
      "topic": "Unclear similarity"
    },
    {
      "quote": "We show that, overall, dogs are an excellent model for many human cancers and that dogs tend to be better models of adult cancers than pediatric cancers, with notable exceptions such as gliomas and sarcomas.",
      "topic": "Overall findings"
    },
    {
      "quote": "Our scalable approach enables rapid and accurate identification of model systems for studying human cancers, creating new opportunities for comparative oncology studies across the tree of life.",
      "topic": "Scalable approach"
    }
  ]
}
